   406			 Supplementary Methods
   407			 S1. BWTandem Parameter Specifications
   408			 S1.1 Tier 1: Short Tandem Repeat Detection
   409			 A Tier 1 candidate of period p and length L base pairs is retained when both of the following hold.
   410	
   411			 L ≥ max(L_min, p · C_min(p)) and Q = L · (1 − e) ≥ Q_min
   412	
   413			 Here e is the mismatch rate, Q is the quality score used throughout the pipeline, and C_min is a period-dependent minimum copy count; the first inequality is tested on the extended seed span, the second on the refined call as reported.
   414	
   415			 C_min(p) = max(2, ⌊6/p⌋ + 2)
   416	
   417			 This gives 8 copies for mononucleotide repeats, 5 for dinucleotides, 4 for trinucleotides, 3 for periods of 4 to 6 bp, and 2 for 7 to 9 bp. The implementation's built-in defaults are max(3, ⌊12/p⌋ + 3); the values above are the settings used for every run reported in this paper, applied through the TIER1_COPYBASE, TIER1_COPYADD and TIER1_MIN_COPIES environment overrides.
   418	
   419			 The length floor L_min and score floor Q_min are 17 for the runs using the relaxed short-period gate and 20 for the BWTandem-P configuration, which omits it. The copy-count term is therefore not the binding constraint for most periods: at L_min = 17, a mononucleotide array must reach 17 copies rather than 8, a dinucleotide 9 rather than 5, and a trinucleotide 6 rather than 4. The copy count binds at p = 6 and p = 9, where p · C_min(p) = 18 exceeds the 17 bp floor; under BWTandem-P's floor of 20 it never binds. A reader tuning sensitivity should treat the length and score floors as the primary levers, not the copy-count formula.
   420	
   421			 In the configuration used throughout this paper the FM-index enumeration runs as a second, additive pass: the sliding-window scanner runs first under the gate above, and the enumeration then scans only the residual sequence that pass did not already claim, so it contributes the diverged short arrays an exact-seed scan cannot reach without double-reporting the ones it can. It enumerates every primitive motif rotation of length 1 to 6 bp, rotations not being collapsed to a canonical form, and applies its own acceptance gate rather than the one above: at least three occurrences of the motif, a span of at least 20 bp, gaps of at most two motif copies within a progression, a log-likelihood ratio for periodic hit testing of 6.0 against an independent-bases null whose base frequencies are estimated from a subsample of up to two million bases, a minimum density of 0.45, and a per-motif cap of 2×10⁷ occurrences on the chromosome being processed, above which the motif is skipped.
   422			 S1.2 Tier 2: Medium-Length Repeat Detection
   423			 In the LCP-driven phase, the detected motif is reduced to its primitive period using exact divisibility checks and approximate periodicity tests with a tolerance of 2%, implemented via autocorrelation.
   424	
   425			 In the BWT k-mer seeding phase, the k-mer and stride formulas resolve to 9 bp k-mers sampled every 5 bp for the 10–50 bp scan and 10 bp k-mers every 10 bp for the long-unit phase, and progressions are accepted within a 3% jitter tolerance. The LCP thresholds are 10 for the long-unit phase and 8 for the 10–50 bp scan's LCP pre-pass. The mismatch tolerance for Tier 2 was set to 30% in every run reported here, against a library default of 20%, and the same rate applies to both phases. The seeding phase covers periods of 10–50 bp and the strict LCP phase periods of 20 bp and above.
   426			 S1.3 Tier 3: Adaptive Parameter Computation and Clamping
   427			 The GC-content-dependent mismatch rate is computed as follows.
   428	
   429			 m = 0.15 + 0.10 · |GC − 0.5|
   430	
   431			 The GC term is bounded in practice by genomic composition: |GC − 0.5| reaches 0.5 only at 0% or 100% GC, so the formula's nominal 20% ceiling is unreachable on real sequence. GC is measured per chromosome over the assembled sequence including N runs; evaluated at whole-genome GC the formula gives 0.159 for human, 0.164 for Arabidopsis and 0.153 for maize, and the per-chromosome values in force vary around those with each chromosome's composition.
   432	
   433			 For sequences exceeding 100 Mb, a large-chromosome mode caps the maximum k-mer occurrence count at 500 and imposes a minimum stride of 150 and a minimum k-mer size of 20. These caps are applied before clamping. The parameters are computed per chromosome, and the 100 Mb gate divides the chromosomes analysed here: the occurrence cap of 500 binds only above 100 Mb (every maize chromosome and the larger human ones), while chromosomes below it take the formula value clamped to 1,500 (roughly 730–1,090 on the five Arabidopsis chromosomes and the full 1,500 on mid-sized human chromosomes), so a 90 Mb chromosome receives three times the occurrence budget of a 250 Mb one. The stride and k-mer minima never bind, because the base formulas already exceed them at this scale.
   434	
   435			 All adaptive parameters are then clamped to empirically validated ranges. The rightmost column gives the values in force across the nuclear chromosomes of the three genomes benchmarked here; the Col-CEN organellar sequences ChrC and ChrM take the short-sequence branch and sit outside every range shown (k-mer 12 and 13, stride 20, maximum occurrences 200); where a range is shown, the realized value depends on chromosome length.
   436	
   437	| Parameter | Minimum | Maximum | Values in force here |
   438	|---|--:|--:|--:|
   439	| K-mer size | 12 | 28 | 24–28 (24–25 Arabidopsis, 26–28 human, 28 maize) |
   440	| Sampling stride | 20 | 300 | 300 (lower only on an Arabidopsis chromosome whose prior-tier coverage exceeds ~90%) |
   441	| Max k-mer occurrences | 200 | 1,500 | 500 above 100 Mb; ~730–1,500 below |
   442	| Mismatch rate | 15% | 20% | 15.3–16.4% |
   443	| Period jitter tolerance | 0.02 | 0.04 | 0.02002 at MAXP 100; 0.0204 at MAXP 2,000 |
   444	| Anchor scan, backward units | 20 | 80 | 20–80 |
   445	| Anchor scan, forward units | 200 | 800 | 200–800 |
   446	
   447				 The Tier-3 period jitter tolerance is 0.02 + 0.02(maximum period / 100,000), clamped to the range tabulated above. It therefore remains close to its lower bound in both arms of the range-cost measurement rather than reaching the 0.04 ceiling.
   448	
   449			 One consequence is that the coverage-dependent stride reduction described in Section 2.1.4 is inert at chromosome scale: halving a base stride of n / 40,000 still exceeds the 300 ceiling for any sequence above roughly 24 Mb, so the clamp binds first and the stride is 300 regardless of prior tier coverage.
   450	
   451			 The anchor-based boundary verification threshold for large arrays ranges from 70% to 80% match fraction, as determined by the adaptive parameter computation. For smaller arrays, banded Needleman-Wunsch alignment is applied copy by copy in the 5′-to-3′ direction against a consensus that is updated after each accepted copy, so the procedure is a rolling consensus rather than an independent alignment per copy and its result depends on copy order. Copies are aligned with an indel allowance of max(1, ⌈p/10⌉) and a band half-width of that allowance plus two, so total cost is proportional to copies × p × (⌈p/10⌉ + 2).
   452			 S1.4 Post-Processing: Autocorrelation Identity
   453			 The autocorrelation identity at period p over a window W of length w is defined as follows.
   454	
   455			 A(W, p) = (1 / (w − p)) · Σᵢ₌₀^{w−p−1} 𝟙[W[i] = W[i + p]]
   456	
   457			 where 𝟙 denotes the indicator function. For the satellite gap-fill pass, the default minimum identity threshold is 45%; only uncovered blocks of 300 bp to 100 kb within 50 kb of an anchoring call are examined. At most three 5 kb windows nominate a period, with an early exit at the first period whose identity exceeds 0.80. The nominated period is then verified across the complete block using sliding-window match and valid-base counts, and only contiguous supported segments of at least max(300 bp, twice the period) are emitted. A supporting window must contain at least 80% unambiguous A/C/G/T bases; the catch-all periodicity pass applies the same floor. For the catch-all periodicity pass, windows of twice the candidate period length, subject to a 12 bp floor, are evaluated using a cumulative sum technique, enabling O(n) computation across all positions; accepted stretches must span at least 20 bp, are reported out to the end of the last passing window, and are discarded if more than 30% of the span is already covered by an existing call.
   458			 S1.5 Index Storage
   459			 The suffix array is stored as 32-bit integers and the BWT as an 8-bit character array. Occurrence counts are maintained through checkpointed arrays sampled every 128 positions and stored as 32-bit integers per alphabet symbol (roughly 0.2 bytes per base against the suffix array's four), enabling rank queries with constant-time checkpoint lookups followed by short linear scans.
   460			 S2. Benchmarking: Additional Execution Details
   461			 BWTandem results in this paper come from the nine configuration rows tabulated below, not one. All are environment overrides on top of the tool's built-in defaults, and several differ from those defaults (the FM-index enumeration mode and the catch-all periodicity pass are off by default, and four gate thresholds are relaxed), so reproducing any number in this paper requires setting them explicitly. The satellite gap-fill pass ran at its defaults in every run scored into a table; the Section 3.2 ablation additionally ran three arms from the Arabidopsis configuration in the table below, minus the TIER1_SHORT_PERIOD_MAX / TIER1_SHORT_MIN_ARRAY_LEN / TIER1_SHORT_MIN_SCORE gate (hence its offset from the Table 2 row): that base; the pass disabled by setting SAT_FILL_MIN_IDENTITY=1.01, above the attainable maximum, since the pass has no on/off switch; and the pass's window moved to 400–1,000 bp with SAT_FILL_MIN_PERIOD=400 SAT_FILL_MAX_PERIOD=1000. BWT_INDEX_CACHE was unset in every run scored into a table, including the regenerated Supplementary Table S3 arms, so each built its own index. (The superseded pre-regeneration sweep had shared one cached index; the cache is keyed on a hash of the sequence and re-verified on load, so it cannot change detection output, and no figure from those runs is carried forward.)
   462	
   463			 All configurations share the following base.
   464	
   465			 TIER1_FMSCAN=1 TIER1_FMSCAN_MIN_DENSITY=0.45 TIER1_FMSCAN_MIN_LLR=6.0
   466			 TIER1_MIN_ARRAY_LEN=20 TIER1_MIN_SCORE=20 TIER1_MIN_COPIES=2
   467			 TIER1_COPYBASE=6 TIER1_COPYADD=2 TIER1_EXT_COPIES=2
   468			 TIER2_MISMATCH=0.30 TIER2_SHORT_REQ_COPIES=2
   469	
   470			 All except BWTandem-P add the relaxed short-period acceptance gate.
   471	
   472			 TIER1_SHORT_PERIOD_MAX=9 TIER1_SHORT_MIN_ARRAY_LEN=17 TIER1_SHORT_MIN_SCORE=17
   473	
   474			 The configurations then differ as follows. The command line is `python -m src.main FASTA --min-period 1 --max-period MAXP --threads T --format bed -o OUT -v`, with MAXP and T as given; `--mask` was left at its default (none), which uppercases soft-masked sequence, and `--tiers` at its default of all three. The regenerated whole-genome runs used Python 3.11.14 with numpy 2.3.1; the Cython and C accelerator extensions were compiled and active. The period-100 panel and the identity sweep were produced later, under Python 3.11.15 with numpy 2.4.6, as their deposited provenance records state. At the benchmarked commit (`0363d8b`) the import package was named `src`, hence the literal reproduction command above — reproducing from that pinned commit requires it verbatim. The package was renamed to `bwtandem` before release (commit `5618dcc`), so on the released code the equivalent invocation is `python -m bwtandem.main` (or the installed `bwtandem` entry point) with identical options and environment.
